no structure a specialised method could use is known, so the general-purpose Dormand-Prince 5(4) method was started, with adaptive steps for rtol = 1.00e-8 and atol = 1.00e-11
by t = 0.0418 s the step size had shrunk to 0.00154 s while the solution changed slowly (by only 8.61e-5 relative per step) and the error estimate was only 0.538 of the tolerance: the step was limited by stability, not accuracy (the estimated h*lambda stayed near 3.42 for 15 steps, at Dormand-Prince's stability limit of about 3.3), which means the system is stiff
so from t = 0.0418 s the integration continued from that state with Rodas3, an L-stable Rosenbrock method (order 3, finite-difference Jacobian, no Newton iteration), whose step is set by accuracy alone: it damps the fast decaying components instead of resolving them
took 1378 steps in all: 49 with Dormand-Prince (13 rejected) before the switch and 1329 with Rodas3 (0 rejected) after it
total drifted by at most 8.3e-13 (relative), within the allowed 1.0e-6
